WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
>> I like decision trees.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:07.310
How about you StatQuest?

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.050
Hello. I'm Josh Starmer,

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.750
and welcome to StatQuest.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.010
Today, we're going to talk about

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.370
decision and classification trees,

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.990
and they're going to be clearly explained.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:20.990
Here is a simple decision tree.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:22.590
If a person wants to

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.430
learn about decision trees,

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:27.445
then they should watch this StatQuest.

00:00:27.445 --> 00:00:29.610
In contrast, if a person

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.290
does not want to learn about decision trees,

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:33.990
then check out the latest

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.550
Justin Bieber video instead.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.190
In general, a decision tree

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.430
makes a statement and then makes

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.610
a decision based on

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:43.950
whether or not that statement

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.730
is true or false. It's no big deal.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.610
When a decision tree

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.410
classifies things into categories,

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.415
it's called a classification tree.

00:00:55.415 --> 00:00:57.250
And when a decision tree

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.270
predicts numeric values,

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.810
it's called a regression tree.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:04.710
In this case, we're using diet

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:08.610
to predict a numeric value for mouse size.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.850
Note, for the remainder of this video,

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:15.290
we are going to focus on classification trees.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.930
However, if you want to learn more

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.630
about regression trees, fear not.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.650
There's a whole StatQuest

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.490
dedicated to regression trees.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.475
The link is in the description below.

00:01:27.475 --> 00:01:29.409
Now, here's

00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:32.980
a more complicated classification tree.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:37.940
It combines numeric data with yes/no data.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.000
So it's okay to mix

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.360
data types in the same tree.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.380
Also, notice that

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.620
the tree asks about exercising

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.360
multiple times and that the amount

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.740
of time exercising isn't always the same.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.020
So numeric thresholds can

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.360
be different for the same data.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.776
Lastly,

00:01:58.776 --> 00:02:02.075
the final classifications can be repeated.

00:02:02.075 --> 00:02:05.290
For the most part, classification trees

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.150
are pretty easy to work with.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:11.190
You start at the top and work your way down

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.770
and down until you

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:14.930
get to a point where you

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:16.370
can't go any further,

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.150
and that's how you'll classify something.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.610
Note, so far, I've been

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.630
labeling the arrows with true or false.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.550
But usually, it is just assumed that if

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:30.670
a statement is true, you go to the left.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.870
And if a statement is false,

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.585
you go to the right.

00:02:34.585 --> 00:02:37.060
So sometimes you see

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.800
true and false labels, sometimes you don't.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.663
It's no big deal. No,

00:02:42.663 --> 00:02:45.555
it's the dreaded terminology alert.

00:02:45.555 --> 00:02:47.620
The very top of the tree is

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:50.620
called the root node or just the root.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.560
These are called internal nodes or branches.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.480
Branches have arrows pointing to them,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.720
and they have arrows pointing away from them.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.760
Lastly, these are called

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.140
leaf nodes or just leaves.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.940
Leaves have arrows pointing to them,

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.640
but there are no arrows

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.240
pointing away from them.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.900
Bam. Now that we know how

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.920
to use and interpret classification trees,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.375
let's learn how to build one from raw data.

00:03:20.375 --> 00:03:22.730
This data tells us whether

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:24.930
or not someone loves popcorn,

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.370
whether or not they love soda,

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:31.310
their age, and whether or not they love

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.870
the 1991 Blockbuster Cool

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.770
As Ice, starring Vanilla Ice.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:40.370
So we will use this data to build

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.690
this classification tree that

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.450
predicts whether or not someone

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.645
loves Cool As Ice.

00:03:46.645 --> 00:03:48.810
Now, pretend you've never

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.595
seen this tree before.

00:03:50.595 --> 00:03:52.270
And let's see how to build

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:54.970
a tree starting with just data.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.930
The first thing we do is

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.050
decide whether Loves Popcorn,

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.510
Loves Soda, or Age should be

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:02.790
the question we ask

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.890
at the very top of the tree.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.750
To make that decision,

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:08.170
we'll start by looking at

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.000
how well Loves Popcorn

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.710
predicts whether or not someone

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:13.930
loves Cool As Ice.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:15.770
To do this, we'll make

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:17.770
a super simple tree that only

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:20.470
asks if someone loves popcorn,

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:23.795
and then we'll run the data down the tree.

00:04:23.795 --> 00:04:26.500
For example, the first person

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.020
in the dataset loves popcorn.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.020
So they go to the leaf on the left.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.885
And because they do not love Cool As Ice,

00:04:35.885 --> 00:04:37.640
we'll keep track of that by

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.500
putting a one under the word no.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.260
The second person in the dataset

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.485
also loves popcorn,

00:04:45.485 --> 00:04:48.880
so they also go to the leaf on the left.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.040
And because they also do

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.065
not love Cool As Ice,

00:04:53.065 --> 00:04:55.895
we increment no to two.

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:59.845
The third person does not love popcorn,

00:04:59.845 --> 00:05:02.550
so they go to the leaf on the right.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.575
And because they love Cool As Ice,

00:05:05.575 --> 00:05:08.490
we put a one under the word yes.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.670
Likewise, we run

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:12.690
the remaining rows down the tree,

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:14.530
keeping track of whether or not

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.400
each one loves Cool As Ice.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.710
Bam. Now let's do

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:23.080
the exact same thing for Loves Soda.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.850
Looking at the two little trees,

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:27.330
we see that neither one

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.350
does a perfect job predicting

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:33.755
who will and who will not love Cool As Ice.

00:05:33.755 --> 00:05:35.450
Specifically,

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:37.670
these three leaves contain mixtures

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.050
of people that do and

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:41.665
do not love Cool As Ice.

00:05:41.665 --> 00:05:45.610
Dret, it's another terminology alert.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.690
Because these three leaves

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:49.150
all contain a mixture of

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:52.750
people who do and do not love Cool As Ice,

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.410
they are called impure.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.170
In contrast, this leaf only

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.615
contains people who do not love Cool As Ice.

00:06:01.615 --> 00:06:03.430
Because both leaves and

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.315
the Loves Popcorn tree are

00:06:05.315 --> 00:06:08.230
impure and only one leaf

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.045
in the Loves Soda tree is impure,

00:06:11.045 --> 00:06:13.135
it seems like Loves Soda

00:06:13.135 --> 00:06:14.690
does a better job predicting

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:18.895
who will and who will not love Cool As Ice.

00:06:18.895 --> 00:06:21.590
But it would be nice if we could quantify

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:22.750
the differences between

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:25.660
Loves Popcorn and Loves Soda.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:28.650
The good news is that there are several ways

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:31.805
to quantify the impurity of the leaves.

00:06:31.805 --> 00:06:34.280
One of the most popular methods

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:36.250
is called Gini Impurity,

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.620
but there are also fancy sounding methods

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:41.360
like entropy and information gain.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.696
However, numerically,

00:06:43.696 --> 00:06:46.000
the methods are all quite similar.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.350
So we will focus on Gini Impurity

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:51.200
since not only is it very popular,

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.220
I think it is the most straightforward.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:56.540
So let's start by calculating

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:59.515
the Gini Impurity for Loves Popcorn.

00:06:59.515 --> 00:07:01.220
To calculate the Gini

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.290
Impurity for Loves Popcorn,

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:04.860
we start by calculating

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.290
the Gini Impurity for the individual leaves.

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:12.140
The Gini Impurity for the leaf on the left is

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:16.105
one minus the probability of yes

00:07:16.105 --> 00:07:21.240
squared minus the probability of no squared.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.960
So we start out with one.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.160
Then we subtract the squared

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.600
probability of someone in

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.880
this leaf loving Cool As Ice, which is one,

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:34.385
the number of people in the leaf who loved

00:07:34.385 --> 00:07:36.580
Cool As Ice divided

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.720
by the total number of people in the leaf,

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.940
four, and then the whole term is squared.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:45.180
Lastly, we subtract

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.020
the squared probability of someone in

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:48.760
this leaf not loving

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:51.340
Cool As Ice, which is three,

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:52.580
the number of people in

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.015
the leaf who did not love

00:07:54.015 --> 00:07:56.440
Cool As Ice divided by

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:57.820
the total number of people in

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:00.320
the leaf squared.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:05.120
And when we do the math, we get 0.375.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.220
So let's put 0.375

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:09.140
under the leaf on

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:11.480
the left so we don't forget it.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:14.350
Now let's calculate the Gini Impurity

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.260
for the leaf on the right.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.945
Just like before, we start out with

00:08:18.945 --> 00:08:21.040
one then we subtract

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:22.620
the squared probability of

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.200
someone in this leaf loving

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.420
Cool As Ice and

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:28.440
the squared probability of someone in

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.700
this leaf not loving Cool As Ice.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:36.455
And when we do the math, we get 0.444.

00:08:36.455 --> 00:08:38.510
Now, because the leaf on

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:40.550
the left has four people in it,

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:42.550
and the leaf on the right only

00:08:42.550 --> 00:08:44.210
has three people in it,

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:45.550
the leaves do not

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:47.920
represent the same number of people.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.050
Thus the total Gini Impurity is

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:54.470
the weighted average of the leaf impurities.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:56.590
We start by calculating

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:59.030
the weight for the leaf on the left.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:01.390
The weight for the left leaf

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:03.750
is the total number of people in the leaf,

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.870
four divided by the total number

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.875
of people in both leaves, seven.

00:09:09.875 --> 00:09:12.440
Then we multiply that weight by

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:17.040
its associated Gini Impurity, 0.375.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:19.040
Now we add the weighted

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:21.720
impurity for the leaf on the right,

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.680
which is the total number

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:24.900
of people in the leaf,

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:28.040
three, divided by the total number

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.520
of people in both leaves, seven, times

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:36.100
the associated Gini Impurity, 0.444.

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:41.020
And when we do the math, we get 0.405.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.160
So the Gini Impurity for

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:47.235
Loves Popcorn is 0.405.

00:09:47.235 --> 00:09:50.060
Likewise, the Gini Impurity

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:53.710
for Loves Soda is 0.214.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:55.650
Now we need to calculate

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:58.360
the Gini Impurity for Age.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:00.990
However, because age contains

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:04.530
numeric data and not just yes/no values,

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:06.665
calculating the Gini Impurity

00:10:06.665 --> 00:10:08.590
is a little more involved.

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:11.590
The first thing we do is sort the rows by

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:15.110
age from lowest value to highest value.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:17.650
Then we calculate the average age

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:19.650
for all adjacent people.

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:23.450
Lastly, we calculate the Gini Impurity values

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:25.860
for each average age.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:28.340
For example, to calculate

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.000
the Gini Impurity for the first value,

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:32.900
we put age less than

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:35.940
9.5 in the Root and because

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.060
the only person with age less than

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:41.895
9.5 does not love Cool As Ice,

00:10:41.895 --> 00:10:46.280
we put a zero under yes and a one under no.

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:49.780
Then everyone with age greater than or

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:53.810
equal to 9.5 goes to the leaf on the right.

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:56.770
Now we calculate the Gini Impurity for

00:10:56.770 --> 00:11:00.080
the leaf on the left and get zero.

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:01.790
And this makes sense

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:03.355
because every single person

00:11:03.355 --> 00:11:06.660
in this leaf does not love Cool As Ice,

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.400
so there is no impurity.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:12.230
Then we calculate the Gini Impurity for

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:16.370
the leaf on the right and get 0.5.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:19.110
Now we calculate the weighted average of

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:20.510
the two impurities to get

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:22.685
the total Gini Impurity,

00:11:22.685 --> 00:11:26.150
and we get 0.429.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:29.645
Likewise, we calculate the Gini Impurities

00:11:29.645 --> 00:11:32.470
for all of the other candidate values.

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:37.080
These two candidate thresholds, 15 and 44,

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:39.295
are tied for the lowest impurity,

00:11:39.295 --> 00:11:43.850
0.343, so we can pick either one.

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:46.760
In this case, we'll pick 15.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:49.340
However, remember that we are

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:52.240
comparing Gini Impurity values for Age,

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:55.200
Loves Popcorn, and Loves Soda to

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:56.800
decide which features should

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:59.060
be at the very top of the tree.

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:01.040
Earlier, we calculated

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:02.625
the Gini Impurity values for

00:12:02.625 --> 00:12:06.100
Loves Popcorn and Loves Soda.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:10.215
And now we have the Gini Impurity for Age.

00:12:10.215 --> 00:12:12.480
And because Loves Soda has

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.200
the lowest Gini Impurity overall,

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:17.055
we know that its leaves

00:12:17.055 --> 00:12:18.920
had the lowest impurity.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:22.555
So we put Loves Soda at the top of the tree.

00:12:22.555 --> 00:12:26.890
Bam. Now, the four people

00:12:26.890 --> 00:12:30.310
that love soda go to a node on the left,

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:32.290
and the people that do not

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:35.470
love soda go to a node on the right.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:39.070
Now let's focus on the node on the left.

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:40.990
All four people that

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.820
love soda are in this node.

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:47.775
Three of these people love Cool As Ice,

00:12:47.775 --> 00:12:50.230
and one does not.

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:53.615
So this node is impure.

00:12:53.615 --> 00:12:56.020
So let's see if we can reduce

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:57.940
the impurity by splitting the people

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:02.700
that love soda based on Loves Popcorn or Age.

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:05.420
We'll start by asking the four people that

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:08.900
love soda if they also love popcorn.

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:11.660
Because two of the four people that

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:14.400
love soda also love popcorn,

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.480
they end up in the leaf on the left.

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:20.460
The remaining two people that love soda

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:24.020
but do not love popcorn end up on the right.

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:26.440
And the total Gini Impurity

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:29.540
for this split is 0.25.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:34.480
So let's put 0.25 here so we don't forget.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:36.660
Now we test different

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:39.240
age thresholds just like before.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.580
Only this time, we only

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.865
consider the ages of people who love soda.

00:13:44.865 --> 00:13:46.660
And age less than

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:49.665
12.5 gives us the lowest impurity,

00:13:49.665 --> 00:13:52.480
zero, because both leaves

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:54.540
have no impurity at all.

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.305
So let's put zero here.

00:13:57.305 --> 00:14:01.650
Now, because zero is less than 0.25,

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:03.470
we will use age less than

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:07.290
12.5 to split this node into leaves.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:10.290
Note, these are leaves because there is

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:12.130
no reason to continue splitting

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:14.570
these people into smaller groups.

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:17.690
Likewise, this node, consisting of

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:19.110
the three people who do not

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:21.550
love soda is also a leaf,

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:24.130
because there is no reason to continue

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:27.255
splitting these people into smaller groups.

00:14:27.255 --> 00:14:30.180
Now there is just one last thing we

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:31.400
need to do before we are

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:33.400
done building this tree.

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:35.620
We need to assign output

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:37.600
values for each leaf.

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:40.700
Generally speaking, the output of

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:42.560
a leaf is whatever category

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:44.580
that has the most values.

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:46.600
In other words, because

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:48.160
the majority of the people in

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:51.720
these leaves do not love Cool As Ice,

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:53.680
the output values are,

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:56.925
does not love Cool As Ice.

00:14:56.925 --> 00:14:59.440
And because the majority of the people in

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:01.955
this leaf love Cool As Ice,

00:15:01.955 --> 00:15:06.200
the output value is loves Cool As Ice.

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:07.745
Hooray.

00:15:07.745 --> 00:15:09.470
We finished building a tree from

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:12.810
this data. Double bam.

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:16.350
Now, if someone new comes along and

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:17.690
we want to predict if they

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:19.575
will love Cool As Ice,

00:15:19.575 --> 00:15:22.190
then we run the data down our tree.

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:24.290
And because they love soda,

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:25.870
they go to the left.

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:28.010
And because they are 15,

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:31.330
so age less than 12.5 is false,

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:33.250
they end up in this leaf.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:35.445
And we predict that they will love

00:15:35.445 --> 00:15:39.630
Cool As Ice. Triple bam.

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:43.330
Now that we understand the main ideas of

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:46.230
how to build and use classification trees,

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:49.230
let's discuss one technical detail.

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:52.190
Remember, when we built this tree,

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:53.610
only one person in the

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:56.490
original dataset made it to this leaf.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:59.670
Because so few people made it to this leaf,

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:02.010
it's hard to have confidence that it will do

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:03.230
a great job making

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:05.190
predictions with future data,

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:07.070
and it is possible that we

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:09.315
have overfit the data.

00:16:09.315 --> 00:16:12.350
Note, if the term overfit is new to

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:15.865
you, don't freak out.

00:16:15.865 --> 00:16:18.470
Instead, check out the StatQuest

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:21.310
on bias and variance in machine learning.

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:23.970
Regardless, in practice, there

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:26.830
are two main ways to deal with this problem.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:28.990
One method is called pruning,

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:30.630
and there's a whole StatQuest

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:33.110
dedicated to it, so check it out.

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:35.550
Alternatively, we can put

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:37.270
limits on how trees grow.

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:39.190
For example, by requiring

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:41.510
three or more people per leaf.

00:16:41.510 --> 00:16:44.490
Now we end up with an impure leaf,

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:46.730
but also a better sense of

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:49.170
the accuracy of our prediction because we

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:51.470
know that only 75% of the people

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:54.855
in the leaf loved Cool As Ice.

00:16:54.855 --> 00:16:57.850
Note, even when a leaf is impure,

00:16:57.850 --> 00:16:59.710
we still need an output value

00:16:59.710 --> 00:17:01.470
to make a classification.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:03.490
And since most of the people in

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:05.900
this leaf love cool As Ice,

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:08.075
that will be the output value.

00:17:08.075 --> 00:17:10.980
Also note, when we build a tree,

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:13.460
we don't know in advance if it is better to

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:14.840
require three people per

00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:16.880
leaf or some other number.

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:18.520
So we test different

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:19.800
values with something called

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.540
cross validation and pick

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:23.235
the one that works best.

00:17:23.235 --> 00:17:25.520
And if you don't know what cross validation

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:27.420
is, check out the Quest.

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:29.320
Bam. Now it's time

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:32.575
for some shameless self-promotion.

00:17:32.575 --> 00:17:34.440
If you want to review statistics

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:36.220
and machine learning offline,

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:38.240
check out the StatQuest study guides

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.020
at statquest.org.

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:42.085
There's something for everyone.

00:17:42.085 --> 00:17:44.480
Hooray. We've made it to the end

